  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Winter Weather Advisories are in effect late tonight through\n   Tuesday for central Idaho, the Bitterroot/Sapphire Mountains,\n   and Southwest Montana.\n\n-  A northwest shift in the incoming system may bring an earlier \n   transition to a rain/snow mix for Missoula, the Bitterroot, and\n   Seeley Lake Tuesday morning.\n\n-  Widespread overnight freeze-thaw cycles will create patchy ice\n   on valley roadways Tuesday night into Wednesday morning.\n\nMid and high-level cloud cover streaming in from the southwest is\npreventing widespread dense fog this morning. A progression of \nweak disturbances will bring areas of light rain showers through \ntoday, with snow levels holding between 5,000 and 6,000 feet.\n\nThe primary winter system arrives late tonight through Tuesday. \nThe main frontal deformation band is now expected to shift further\nnorthwest, slowing and stalling over West-Central Montana. This \nshift may introduce an earlier transition to a rain/snow mix \nTuesday morning for the Bitterroot Valley, Missoula, and Seeley- \nSwan, yielding a trace to one inch of slushy accumulation. Minor \nwinter impacts will also extend northward toward Marias Pass.\n\nBy Tuesday afternoon, precipitation will taper across West-\nCentral Montana as the heaviest snowfall rates transition into \nLemhi County and Southwest Montana, including Butte and Homestake \nPass. Winter Weather Advisories remain in effect for the higher \nterrain of central Idaho, the Bitterroot/Sapphire Mountains, and \nSouthwest Montana where plowable snow is expected. Valley roads \nwill generally remain wet and slushy during the day Tuesday, but \ndropping temperatures Tuesday night will result in a widespread \nfreeze-up hazard and patchy ice on road surfaces especially\nbridges and overpasses.\n\nA secondary trough will drop into the region Thursday into Friday,\nmaintaining cooler temperatures and bringing additional\naccumulating mountain snow and a mix of rain and snow for valley\nlocations, primarily south of the I-90 corridor.",
